Expected utility models choices over uncertain wealth; risk preferences concern utility curvature and context.|A risk-averse person prefers certain ₹50 over a fair ₹0/₹100 lottery.|Same expected cash implies equal preference for everyone?|No|Yes|Only mathematically
Prospect theory evaluates gains and losses relative to reference points; loss aversion gives losses greater subjective weight.|A loss feels more painful than an equal gain feels rewarding.|This necessarily describes every investor identically?|No|Yes|Only novices
Anchoring overweights an initial reference; framing changes choices through presentation.|An investor fixates on purchase price instead of future cash prospects.|The relevant decision should emphasise…?|Current alternatives and future outcomes|Purchase price alone|A round-number target
Mental accounting separates money into psychological buckets despite economic fungibility.|An investor treats a dividend as spendable while refusing to sell equivalent value.|The source label changes total wealth automatically?|No|Yes|Only for dividends
Overconfidence overstates skill; self-attribution credits wins internally and losses externally.|A trader calls gains skill and losses bad luck.|A useful corrective is…?|Compare predictions with outcomes systematically|Trade more immediately|Ignore losing periods
Confirmation seeks supportive evidence; availability overweights memorable information.|An analyst reads only favourable reports about a holding.|Improve the process by…?|Seeking disconfirming evidence|Repeating the thesis|Removing contrary sources
Herding follows others without sufficient independent analysis.|Investors buy because peers are buying despite weak cash economics.|Popularity alone establishes fundamental value?|No|Yes|Only at high volume
Recency overweights recent outcomes; extrapolation projects them too far.|Three strong quarters become an assumption of perpetual rapid growth.|Best response?|Check longer history and sustainable drivers|Extend the trend automatically|Ignore competition
Disposition effect is a tendency to realise gains and retain losses for psychological reasons.|A losing investment is held only to return to purchase price.|Evaluate holding based on…?|Future risk-adjusted alternatives|Break-even pride|Original price alone
Present bias overweights immediate rewards; self-control tools support long-term goals.|Automatic contributions reduce temptation to spend earmarked savings.|Automation guarantees suitable asset selection?|No|Yes|Only monthly
Bubbles and panics combine expectations, incentives, financing, and feedback loops.|Rising prices attract leveraged buyers, creating more demand.|Rapid price increases prove sustainable value growth?|No|Yes|Only with high volume
Behavioural corporate finance examines biased managers, investor sentiment, and financing choices.|An overconfident executive overpays for an acquisition.|Manager confidence alone is evidence of synergy value?|No|Yes|Only with experience
Decision journals record assumptions; checklists reduce omissions; pre-mortems imagine failure before commitment.|A team lists reasons a proposed investment could disappoint.|The purpose is…?|Expose assumptions and prevent avoidable errors|Guarantee success|Replace analysis entirely
Incentives influence choices; decision quality should be judged using information available at the time, not outcomes alone.|A sound decision loses after an unforeseeable shock.|A bad outcome proves a bad process?|No|Yes|Only in investing
